  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B005",
        "laureates": [
          "Antoine Georges",
          "Dieter Vollhardt",
          "Gabriel Kotliar"
        ],
        "citation": "For the development of dynamical mean-field theory for strongly correlated electrons."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B006",
    "B018"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Dynamical mean-field theory gave physicists a broadly useful framework for treating strongly correlated electrons, a central problem where ordinary approaches can fail. Its long record of development and application makes the theoretical achievement mature and consequential. Georges, Vollhardt, and Kotliar are identified as central architects of the theory and its influential formulation.",
    "case_against": "The method grew through collaborative work, and a prize to three people may compress a broader intellectual history. Its achievement is a theoretical framework rather than a discrete experimental discovery, so the citation must keep the scope precise and avoid implying that these three alone developed all of correlated-electron physics.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson describes DMFT as a broadly useful framework whose development involved a wide community, and questions whether it is a singular discovery as sharply defined as an experimental result.",
        "response": "That is a real attribution and framing concern. I would bound the recognition to the development of DMFT itself, rather than the wider study of correlated materials. A theoretical framework can be a distinct achievement when it makes a difficult class of problems tractable and has a sustained record of use."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth likewise notes DMFT's long-established value, while finding its landmark advances harder to summarize as one discovery than a discrete result.",
        "response": "I agree that the citation needs care. Here the method's central framework provides a coherent object of recognition, even though its development was collaborative. The distinction between a framework and a single observation is not by itself a reason to discount its scientific contribution."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The precise division of foundational contributions among Georges, Vollhardt, Kotliar, and the wider community needs careful historical review."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No candidate-specific connection apparent from the supplied profile; it notes institutional proximity to committee colleagues Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson."
  }
}
